e Node 40-8 ][93]\n    * [Direction Finding] Direction finding\n      * [ Direction finding overview ][94]\n      * [ RFeye Array 300 Series ][95]\n      * [ RFeye Array 100 Series ][96]\n      * [ Compact DF heads overview ][97]\n      * [ Spectrum monitoring antennas ][98]\n      * [ DFH500 ][99]\n      * [ DFH18 ][100]\n      * [ DFH40 ][101]\n    * [Deployments] Deployments\n      * [ Unmanned systems ][102]\n      * [ Integrated vehicles ][103]\n      * [ Integrated drones ][104]\n      * [ RFeye Stormcase ][105]\n      * [ Outdoor System Kit ][106]\n      * [ Mini Outdoor System Kit ][107]\n    * [TSCM] TSCM\n      * [ TSCM overview ][108]\n      * [ RFeye Guard ][109]\n    * [RF recording IQ] RF recording (I/Q)\n      * [ RF recording (I/Q) overview ][110]\n      * [ RFeye Node Plus 100-18 ][111]\n      * [ RFeye SenS Portable ][112]\n  * Software\n    * [RFeye Suite] RFeye Suite\n      * [ Software overview ][113]\n      * [ RFeye Site ][114]\n      * [ RFeye Mission Manager ][115]\n      * [ RFeye DeepView ][116]\n      * [ Software releases ][117]\n      * [ Software Support & Updates Service ][118]\n* [ Resources ][119]\n  * * [Blog][120]\n    * [Resources][121]\n    * [Knowledge base][122]\n* [ Newsroom ][123]\n  * * [Events][124]\n    * [Company news][125]\n* [ Expertise ][126]\n  * * [Deployment stories][127]\n    * [Making sense of RF][128]\n    * [A guide to RF geolocation][129]\n    * [A guide to EMSO][130]\n* [ About ][131]\n  * * [About CRFS][132]\n    * [Careers][133]\n* [Partners][134]\n* [Customer support][135]\n* [Talk to us][136]\n* [Customer Support][137]\n* [Partners][138]\n\n# EXTRAORDINARY RF TECHNOLOGY\n\n##### Helping you understand & exploit the electromagnetic environment\n\n[Talk to us][139]\n[The RFeye ecosystem][140]\n[Green_Node_Transp_no_shadow_ai-2]\n[Multi-mission sensors ]\n\n###### Multi-mission sensors\n\nPowerful, ruggedized, and optimized for SWaP, CRFS sensors are multifunctional, allowing them to be\nrepurposed or interchanged across missions and applications.\n[Multi-domain superiority]\n\n###### Multi-domain superiority\n\nCRFS passive RF technology supports collaborative intelligence networks and integration across the\nair, land, sea, spaceport, and cyber domains to overcome modern threats.\n\nAPPLICATIONS OVERVIEW\n\n### Obtain RF intelligence, maintain spectrum awareness, and achieve mission-critical objectives\n\n### Who we help\n\n##### Military & EW operators\n\n**Military & EW operators**\n\nActionable RF intelligence in contested electromagnetic environments to help military forces gain\nspectrum dominance.\n\n[Read more][141]\n\n##### Spectrum regulators\n\n**Spectrum regulators**\n\nITU-compliant solutions for real-time spectrum monitoring, interference hunting, geolocation, major\nevent management, and more.\u00a0\n\n[Read more][142]\n\n##### Security & intelligence agencies\n\n**Security & intelligence agencies**\n\nActionable RF intelligence and counter surveillance solutions to fight crime, cyber, and homeland\nsecurity threats.\u00a0\n\n[Read more][143]\n\n##### Defense & system integrators\n\n**Defense & system integrators**\n\nAdvanced COTS RF sensor technology adds advanced capabilities, allowing military end users to\nunderstand and exploit the EM environment and achieve spectrum dominance.\n\n\n[Read more][144]\n\nTalk to an advisor to discuss how CRFS can enhance your RF capabilities\n\n[Talk to us][145]\n\n##### CRFS special reports\n\n[ [Forewarned is Forearmed Home] ][146]\n\n###### Forewarned is Forearmed\n\nUsing wide area, networked, radio frequency sensing to enhance regional defence and security in the\nMiddle East. This special report by Dr Thomas Withington joins seemingly disparate threats across\nthe region and offers a framework for how militaries and security agencies can augment their\ndefences by adding a layer of RF protection.\n\n[Read the report][147]\n[ [Competition to Conflict Home] ][148]\n\n###### Competition to Conflict\n\nThe Asia-Pacific is the frontline between competition & conflict. Grey-zone tactics, increased\nmilitarization, and border disputes are reshaping the region\u2019s security landscape. Dr Thomas\nWithington\u2019s special report examines how electromagnetic sensing can strengthen deterrence, enhance\nsituational awareness, and prevent escalation.\u00a0\n\n[Read the report][149]\n[ [Hertz and Minds Home] ][150]\n\n###### Hertz & Minds\n\nDr Withington\u2019s in-depth analysis highlights essential lessons from the conflict for future warfare.\nNATO forces can no longer assume spectrum dominance and will likely depend on passive, networked,\nunattended sensors across the battlefield.\n\n[Read the report][151]\n\n#### Solutions\n\n1/1\n1/1\n1/1\n[Spectrum monitoring]\n\n##### Spectrum monitoring\n\nProactive, automated monitoring means any jamming, interference, or breach of conditions can be\nquickly identified and corrective actions taken.\u00a0\n\n[Continue reading][152]\n[DF & geolocation]\n\n##### Direction finding & geolocation\n\nKnowing the location of a transmission can deliver vital intelligence and critical insights for\neffective decision-making\u2014for both military and civilian applications.\u00a0\n\n[Continue reading][153]\n[Military-EMSO-icon]\n\n##### Military spectrum & EMSO\n\nDetecting, identifying, and locating enemy RF emissions enhances situational awareness and ensures\nspectrum dominance\u2014to disrupt hostile operations and protect friendly forces.\u00a0\n\n[Continue reading][154]\n[Border-icon]\n\n##### Border security monitoring\n\nPassively monitoring vast areas, land-border crossings, ports, and coastal areas helps identify\nthreats, and systems can be integrated into security and EW operations.\u00a0\n\n[Continue reading][155]\n[Infrastructure-protection-icon]\n\n##### Infrastructure protection\n\nA network of RF receivers allows physical, electronic, cyber, and human threats to be detected and\ngeolocated\u2014ensuring the integrity of critical infrastructure.\u00a0\u00a0\n\n[Continue reading][156]\n[RF-recording-icon]\n\n##### Air defense\n\nPassive, wide-area 3D TDoA extends reach beyond radar to identify and geolocate transmissions from\nairborne targets while remaining invisible to electronic detection.\u00a0\u00a0\n\n[Continue reading][157]\n[Drone-detection-icon]\n\n##### Drone detection\n\nPassively monitoring drones with an RF sensor system allows operators to geolocate individual\ndrones, drone swarms, and drone operator emissions in 3D in real time. \u00a0\n\n[Continue reading][158]\n[RF-recording-icon-1]\n\n##### RF recording & signal capture\n\nFinding and recording signals of interest in high-fidelity maximizes the intelligence picture,\naccelerates R&D roadmaps, and provides valuable RF intelligence for EM superiority.\u00a0\n\n[Continue reading][159]\n\n### Hardware & software solutions\n* [\n  [Receivers] [Receivers]\n  \n  ### RF sensors (Nodes)\n  \n  High-performance RF sensors that can be used for fixed, mobile-tactical, or in-building\n  deployments\n  \n  ][160]\n  \n  #### RF sensors (Nodes)\n  \n  RFeye Nodes can be used for fixed, mobile-tactical, or in-building deployments. They are powerful,\n  portable, and rugged RF sensors built for any environment that receive and record signals and\n  geolocation of transmitters.\n  \n  [RF sensors overview][161]\n  \n  [Tab Image]\n* [\n  [Direction Finding] [Direction Finding (DF)]\n  \n  ### Direction Finding (DF)\n  \n  Ultra-wide frequency, high-performance radio direction finding\n  \n  ][162]\n  \n  #### Direction Finding (DF)\n  \n  Ultra-wide frequency, high-performance radio direction finding that synchronously uses TDoA and\n  DF. These techniques can be used in parallel to find the most accurate geolocation.\u00a0CRFS\u2019 advanced\n  DF and geolocation solutions perform multi-later, multi-band scans up to 40 GHz.\u00a0\n  \n  [Direction finding overview][163]\n  \n  [Tab Image]\n* [\n  [I/Q Data Recorders] [I/Q Data Recorders]\n  \n  ### RF recording (I/Q capture)\n  \n  Find, capture, record, and analyze long-duration, wideband RF recordings in high fidelity for\n  enhanced intelligence\n  \n  ][164]\n  \n  #### RF recorders (I/Q capture)\n  \n  Find, capture, record, and replay long-duration wideband RF recordings in high fidelity for\n  enhanced intelligence.\u00a0Create I/Q data snippets from digitalized RF recordings to view, slice,\n  stream, and demodulate.\u00a0\n  \n  [RF recorder overview][165]\n  \n  [Tab Image]\n* [\n  [Integrations] [Integ"
          }
        ]
